WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.040
A neuron's cell body and its axon share one cytoplasm.

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.140
Both contain caspases.

3
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.760
So how can caspases run in one and not the other?

4
00:00:13.783 --> 00:00:19.483
Caspases are proteases that dismantle a cell during apoptosis, a built-in program of

5
00:00:19.483 --> 00:00:20.483
cell death.

6
00:00:20.967 --> 00:00:24.247
To find out, you must deprive one place and not the other.

7
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:31.070
In 2013, Corey Cusack, Mohanish Deshmukh and colleagues grew mouse sympathetic

8
00:00:31.070 --> 00:00:32.910
neurons in a microfluidic chamber.

9
00:00:33.983 --> 00:00:36.183
The cell bodies sat in one compartment.

10
00:00:37.143 --> 00:00:39.663
Their axons grew through narrow grooves into a second.

11
00:00:40.483 --> 00:00:41.623
The fluids stayed apart.

12
00:00:42.583 --> 00:00:47.343
Local deprivation removes NGF from the axon compartment only.

13
00:00:48.303 --> 00:00:50.303
Global deprivation removes it from both.

14
00:00:51.283 --> 00:00:54.323
In normal neurons, what happens in each experiment?

15
00:01:00.433 --> 00:01:04.873
Local deprivation: the axons degenerate, and the cell bodies live.

16
00:01:05.653 --> 00:01:06.913
That is pruning, in a dish.

17
00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:12.257
Global deprivation: the neurons die, and their axons go with them.

18
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:15.217
That is developmental cell death, in a dish.

19
00:01:16.233 --> 00:01:18.933
Now fill in a table, one genotype per row.

20
00:01:19.917 --> 00:01:23.337
Does axon breakdown need caspase-6 in both experiments?

21
00:01:24.617 --> 00:01:25.977
Delete it, and predict each one.

22
00:01:31.917 --> 00:01:35.757
Without caspase-6, locally deprived axons were protected.

23
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.580
After global deprivation, the axons still degenerated as the neurons died.

24
00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.840
So the two experiments have different needs.

25
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.080
A second mutant shows whether they are two programs.

26
00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.813
Now the other end of the pathway.

27
00:01:54.833 --> 00:01:58.413
During apoptosis, the protein Apaf-1 builds the apoptosome.

28
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:05.013
The apoptosome switches on Caspase nine then switches on caspase-3.

29
00:02:06.017 --> 00:02:10.297
Local pruning needed caspase-9, caspase-3 and caspase-6.

30
00:02:11.137 --> 00:02:12.357
Does it need Apaf-1 too?

31
00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:24.850
No. Without Apaf-1, locally deprived axons still degenerated, and the cell bodies

32
00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:25.850
lived.

33
00:02:26.233 --> 00:02:29.893
But after global deprivation, these neurons resisted death.

34
00:02:30.793 --> 00:02:32.853
Death of the whole neuron needed Apaf-1.

35
00:02:36.817 --> 00:02:40.737
So in this study, caspase-6 mattered only for the local route.

36
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:43.857
Apaf-1 mattered only for the global route.

37
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.060
Each route needs something the other does not.

38
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.420
They share enzymes, but they are wired differently.

39
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.480
Caspases become active in the deprived axon.

40
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.280
What keeps their activity low in the cell body?

41
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:02.353
The same study found two things that keep caspases off in the cell body.

42
00:03:03.233 --> 00:03:07.413
One is XIAP, which blocks active caspase-9 and caspase-3.

43
00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.047
The other is the proteasome.

44
00:03:11.017 --> 00:03:15.817
Neurons lacking XIAP built up active caspase-3 in their cell bodies.

45
00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:24.450
Same enzymes, two wirings, and an inhibitor guarding the part that must live.
